## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical AWS knowledge of private content delivery, CloudFront with OAI, and Lambda@Edge integration, plus a tangible test plan.

## Key Concepts
- CloudFront Origin Access Identity (OAI)
- S3 bucket policies restricting GetObject to OAI only
- TLS enforcement and viewer protocol policy
- Lambda@Edge for header augmentation
- End-to-end testing of CDN delivery vs direct S3 access

## Code Example
```javascript
'use strict';
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const request = event.Records[0].cf.request;
  const tenantId = (request.headers['x-tenant-id'] && request.headers['x-tenant-id'][0].value) || 'unknown';
  request.headers['X-Tenant-Id'] = [{ key: 'X-Tenant-Id', value: tenantId }];
  return request;
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you rotate the CloudFront OAI and keys securely?
- How would you test the bucket policy with simulated access from non-CloudFront principals?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests comprehensive data architecture skills including cross-account streaming, centralized analytics, security controls, disaster recovery, and governance at enterprise scale.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-account IAM role design with least privilege principles
- Kinesis Data Streams vs MSK trade-offs for multi-tenant workloads
- Lake Formation cell-based security and column-level access controls
- Server-side encryption with KMS using per-tenant customer-managed keys
- Multi-region disaster recovery and automated schema evolution strategies

## Code Example

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": [
        "kinesis:DescribeStream",
        "kinesis:GetRecords",
        "kinesis:GetShardIterator",
        "kinesis:ListStreams"
      ],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:kinesis:*:ACCOUNT_ID:stream/TENANT_*"
    },
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": [
        "s3:GetObject",
        "s3:ListBucket"
      ],
      "Resource": [
        "arn:aws:s3:::central-data-lake/*",
        "arn:aws:s3:::central-data-lake"
      ],
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {
          "s3:ExistingObjectTag/TenantId": "${aws:userid}"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests proficiency designing fine-grained, scalable access controls for multi-tenant data lakes using ABAC, tags, and policy syntax.

## Key Concepts
- IAM tags and aws:PrincipalTag
- Resource policies with aws:RequestTag and stringEquals conditions
- S3 bucket prefixes per tenant and least privilege
- Testing cross-tenant access and tag revocation

## Code Example
```json
// Trust policy (IAM role)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT:role/TenantAccess"},
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id": "${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}"}}
    }
  ]
}
```
```json
// Permission policy
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::data-lake/${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}/*",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"aws:RequestTag/tenant-id": "${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}"}}
    }
  ]
}
```
```json
// Bucket policy (alternative strict guard)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT:role/TenantAccess"},
      "Action": "s3:GetObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::data-lake/${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}/*",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"s3:ExistingObjectTag/tenant-id": "${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you rotate tags and ensure no stale access remains?
- How would you integrate with CI to validate policies in a sandbox before prod?
